{"task_id": "Mbpp/11", "solution": "def remove_Occ(text: str, char: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Removes the first and last occurrence of a given character from the string.\n    Returns a new string with the occurrences removed.\n    \"\"\"\n    first_idx = text.find(char)\n    last_idx = text.rfind(char)\n\n    if first_idx == -1:\n        # If the character is not found, return the original string.\n        return text\n    elif first_idx == last_idx:\n        # If the character is found only once, remove that single occurrence.\n        return text[:first_idx] + text[first_idx + 1:]\n    else:\n        # If the character is found multiple times, remove the first and last occurrences.\n        return text[:first_idx] + text[first_idx + 1:last_idx] + text[last_idx + 1:]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/3", "solution": "def is_not_prime(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies if a given integer is a non-prime number.\n    Returns True for non-prime numbers and False for prime numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number <= 1:\n        return True  # Numbers less than or equal to 1 are not prime.\n    if number == 2:\n        return False # 2 is prime, so it's not non-prime.\n    if number % 2 == 0:\n        return True  # All other even numbers are not prime.\n\n    # Check for odd divisors from 3 up to the square root of the number.\n    i = 3\n    while i * i <= number:\n        if number % i == 0:\n            return True # Found an odd divisor, so it's not prime.\n        i += 2\n    \n    return False # No divisors found, the number is prime."}
