{"task_id": "Mbpp/126", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Iterator\n\ndef sum(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of common positive divisors of two integers.\n    This function implements the core logic described in the design document.\n    \"\"\"\n    _validate_inputs(a, b)\n\n    if a == 0 or b == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    return _compute_sum_of_divisors(a, b)\n\ndef _validate_inputs(a: int, b: int) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Validates that inputs are integers and are non-negative.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(a, int) or not isinstance(b, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Inputs must be integers.\")\n    if a < 0 or b < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Inputs must be non-negative.\")\n\ndef _common_divisors(a: int, b: int) -> Iterator[int]:\n    \"\"\"Yields all common positive divisors of two non-negative integers.\"\"\"\n    # Find the smaller of the two numbers to limit the search space.\n    # The greatest common divisor (GCD) approach or iterating up to min(a,b)\n    # are common strategies. We'll iterate up to min(a,b).\n    # Note: This is a direct implementation as per common divisor finding.\n    # The reference to \"Euclid's algorithm for efficiency\" in the design was\n    # for GCD calculation which can then find divisors of GCD.\n    # For now, we take the straightforward approach for finding all common divisors.\n    # If a or b is zero, _validate_inputs handles it, so we assume non-zero here.\n    \n    if a == 0 or b == 0: # Redundant check if _validate_inputs ensures non-zero, but safe.\n        return\n\n    min_val = min(a, b)\n    for i in range(1, min_val + 1):\n        if a % i == 0 and b % i == 0:\n            yield i\n\ndef _compute_sum_of_divisors(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Computes the sum of common divisors from the generator.\"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    for divisor in _common_divisors(a, b):\n        total_sum += divisor\n    return total_sum\n```"}
