{"task_id": "Mbpp/237", "solution": "def check_occurences(list_of_tuples):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the occurrences of unique normalized tuples in a list.\n    Tuples (a, b) and (b, a) are considered the same and normalized to (min(a, b), max(a, b)).\n    \"\"\"\n    from collections import Counter\n\n    normalized_counts = Counter()\n    for t in list_of_tuples:\n        normalized_t = tuple(sorted(t))\n        normalized_counts[normalized_t] += 1\n    return dict(normalized_counts)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/235", "solution": "def even_bit_set_number(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets all even-indexed bits of the given non-negative number to 1.\n    Even bit positions are 0, 2, 4, ... (least significant bit is 0).\n    \"\"\"\n    mask = 0\n    # Determine the number of bits needed to represent the number.\n    # We generate a mask that covers at least these bits.\n    # The mask will have 1s at even positions and 0s at odd positions.\n    num_bits = number.bit_length()\n    \n    # Iterate through even bit positions up to the number's bit length.\n    # If num_bits is 0 (for input 0), the range is empty, mask remains 0.\n    for i in range(0, num_bits + 1, 2):\n        mask |= (1 << i)\n            \n    # OR the number with the generated mask.\n    # This ensures all even bits (as defined by the mask) are set to 1,\n    # and odd bits of the original number remain unchanged.\n    return number | mask"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of sequences of length n, where each element is\n    a positive integer, at least twice the previous, and no more than m.\n    \"\"\"\n    # dp_prev_row[j] stores the number of sequences of length (current_length - 1)\n    # ending with the value j. We use 1-based indexing for values.\n    dp_prev_row = [0] * (m + 1)\n\n    # Base case: For sequences of length 1, any integer from 1 to m is a valid sequence.\n    for val in range(1, m + 1):\n        dp_prev_row[val] = 1\n\n    # Iterate for sequence length from 2 up to n.\n    for current_length in range(2, n + 1):\n        dp_curr_row = [0] * (m + 1)\n        \n        # Calculate prefix sums for dp_prev_row to optimize the inner summation.\n        # prefix_sum_prev[k] stores the sum of dp_prev_row[j] for j from 1 to k.\n        prefix_sum_prev = [0] * (m + 1)\n        current_sum_for_prefix = 0\n        for val_idx in range(1, m + 1):\n            current_sum_for_prefix += dp_prev_row[val_idx]\n            prefix_sum_prev[val_idx] = current_sum_for_prefix\n\n        # Fill dp_curr_row for the current_length.\n        for current_val in range(1, m + 1):\n            # A previous element (prev_val) must satisfy: current_val >= 2 * prev_val\n            # This implies prev_val <= current_val / 2.\n            # So, prev_val can range from 1 up to current_val // 2.\n            max_prev_val_allowed = current_val // 2\n            \n            # If there are valid previous elements (i.e., max_prev_val_allowed is at least 1),\n            # sum up the counts from the previous length's valid ending values.\n            if max_prev_val_allowed >= 1:\n                dp_curr_row[current_val] = prefix_sum_prev[max_prev_val_allowed]\n            # If max_prev_val_allowed is 0 (i.e., current_val < 2), no valid previous elements exist,\n            # so dp_curr_row[current_val] remains 0.\n\n        # The current_row becomes the previous_row for the next length iteration.\n        dp_prev_row = dp_curr_row\n\n    # The total number of sequences of length n is the sum of all elements in dp_prev_row,\n    # which now holds the counts for sequences of length n.\n    total_count = sum(dp_prev_row)\n    return total_count"}
