{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def max_sum(array):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence for the given array.\n    A sequence is bitonic if it is first increasing and then decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(array)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return float('-inf') # Return negative infinity if no elements can form a sum.\n\n    # dp_inc[i] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    dp_inc = [0] * n\n    # dp_dec[i] stores the maximum sum of a decreasing subsequence starting at index i.\n    dp_dec = [0] * n\n\n    # Compute dp_inc values\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp_inc[i] = array[i]\n        for j in range(i):\n            if array[j] < array[i]:\n                dp_inc[i] = max(dp_inc[i], dp_inc[j] + array[i])\n\n    # Compute dp_dec values\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        dp_dec[i] = array[i]\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if array[j] < array[i]:\n                dp_dec[i] = max(dp_dec[i], dp_dec[j] + array[i])\n\n    # Combine dp_inc and dp_dec to find the maximum bitonic sum.\n    # For each element array[i], consider it as the peak of a bitonic subsequence.\n    # The sum will be dp_inc[i] + dp_dec[i] - array[i] (subtract array[i] as it's counted twice).\n    max_overall_sum = float('-inf')\n    for i in range(n):\n        current_bitonic_sum = dp_inc[i] + dp_dec[i] - array[i]\n        max_overall_sum = max(max_overall_sum, current_bitonic_sum)\n\n    return max_overall_sum"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "def lps(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the given string.\n    Uses dynamic programming with O(n) space complexity.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[j] stores the length of the longest palindromic subsequence for the\n    # substring s[current_i...j].\n    # When calculating for a new 'current_i', dp[j] initially holds the\n    # value for s[current_i+1...j].\n    dp = [0] * n\n\n    # Iterate 'i' from the last character down to the first\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        # old_dp_j_minus_1 stores dp[i+1][j-1] from the conceptual 2D table.\n        # It's the diagonal value from the previous 'i' row.\n        # For j=i, this corresponds to dp[i+1][i-1], which is 0.\n        old_dp_j_minus_1 = 0\n        # Iterate 'j' from 'i' up to the last character\n        for j in range(i, n):\n            # Save dp[i+1][j] (the current value of dp[j]) as it will become\n            # old_dp_j_minus_1 for the next 'j' iteration.\n            temp = dp[j]\n\n            if i == j:\n                # Base case: A single character is a palindrome of length 1.\n                dp[j] = 1\n            elif s[i] == s[j]:\n                # If characters match, add 2 (for s[i] and s[j]) to the\n                # LPS of the inner substring s[i+1...j-1].\n                dp[j] = 2 + old_dp_j_minus_1\n            else:\n                # If characters don't match, take the maximum of:\n                # 1. LPS of s[i+1...j] (which is 'temp', the old dp[j])\n                # 2. LPS of s[i...j-1] (which is 'dp[j-1]', already computed for current 'i')\n                dp[j] = max(temp, dp[j-1])\n\n            # Update old_dp_j_minus_1 for the next iteration of 'j'\n            old_dp_j_minus_1 = temp\n            \n    # The final answer is the LPS for the entire string s[0...n-1], stored in dp[n-1].\n    return dp[n-1]"}
