{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def max_product(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum product formed by multiplying numbers of an increasing subsequence.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i] stores the maximum product of an increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n    dp = [0] * n\n    \n    # Initialize dp values. Each element itself is an increasing subsequence.\n    # We use a variable to track the overall maximum product found.\n    max_overall_product = 0\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i] = arr[i] # Current element itself forms an increasing subsequence\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                # If arr[i] can extend the increasing subsequence ending at arr[j],\n                # consider the product dp[j] * arr[i].\n                # We take the maximum of current dp[i] and this new product.\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] * arr[i])\n        \n        # Update the overall maximum product found so far.\n        max_overall_product = max(max_overall_product, dp[i])\n        \n    return max_overall_product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/471", "solution": "def find_remainder(numbers: list[int], modulus: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the product of a list of integers modulo a given modulus.\n    Returns 0 if the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0\n\n    product = 1\n    for num in numbers:\n        product = (product * num) % modulus\n\n    return product"}
