{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "def check_Consecutive(elements: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a list contains numbers that form a consecutive sequence.\n    This implementation runs in O(n) time by checking the range and uniqueness.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(elements)\n    if n <= 1:\n        # An empty list or a list with a single element is not considered consecutive.\n        return False\n\n    # Find the minimum and maximum values in the list.\n    min_val = min(elements)\n    max_val = max(elements)\n\n    # For a list of `n` consecutive numbers, the difference between max and min\n    # must be exactly `n - 1`.\n    if (max_val - min_val + 1) != n:\n        return False\n\n    # Additionally, all numbers must be unique. A set can check this efficiently.\n    # If the length of the set is not equal to the original list length, there are duplicates.\n    if len(set(elements)) != n:\n        return False\n\n    return True"}
