{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "import math\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef perfect_squares(lower: int, upper: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Finds all perfect squares within a given inclusive integer range.\n    Handles negative lower bounds by starting from 0 and returns an empty list if no perfect squares exist or if the range is invalid.\n    \"\"\"\n    if lower > upper:\n        return []\n\n    adjusted_lower = max(0, lower)\n    perfect_squares_list = []\n\n    # Determine the smallest integer 'start_i' whose square is greater than or equal to adjusted_lower.\n    start_sqrt_candidate = math.isqrt(adjusted_lower)\n    if start_sqrt_candidate * start_sqrt_candidate < adjusted_lower:\n        start_i = start_sqrt_candidate + 1\n    else:\n        start_i = start_sqrt_candidate\n\n    # Determine the largest integer 'end_i' whose square is less than or equal to upper.\n    end_i = math.isqrt(upper)\n\n    # Generate perfect squares by squaring integers from start_i to end_i.\n    for i in range(start_i, end_i + 1):\n        square = i * i\n        perfect_squares_list.append(square)\n\n    return perfect_squares_list"}
