{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "def get_median(list1, list2, size):\n    # Calculates the median of two sorted lists of the same size.\n    # It iteratively merges elements to find the (size)-th and (size+1)-th elements.\n    i = 0  # Pointer for list1\n    j = 0  # Pointer for list2\n    m1 = 0 # Stores the element at (size - 1) index (0-based)\n    m2 = 0 # Stores the element at (size) index (0-based)\n    count = 0\n\n    # Iterate until we have processed enough elements to find m1 and m2\n    while count <= size:\n        current_val = 0\n        # If list1 is exhausted, take from list2\n        if i == size:\n            current_val = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n        # If list2 is exhausted, take from list1\n        elif j == size:\n            current_val = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        # Compare elements and take the smaller one\n        elif list1[i] < list2[j]:\n            current_val = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            current_val = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n\n        # The element at 0-indexed position (size - 1) is the (size)-th smallest element\n        if count == size - 1:\n            m1 = current_val\n        # The element at 0-indexed position (size) is the (size+1)-th smallest element\n        if count == size:\n            m2 = current_val\n\n        count += 1\n\n    return (float(m1) + float(m2)) / 2.0"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "from typing import List\n\nclass NumberList:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates a list of integers.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, numbers: List[int]):\n        self.numbers = numbers\n\n    def size(self) -> int:\n        return len(self.numbers)\n\n\nclass Subset:\n    \"\"\"Represents a subset of integers.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, elements: List[int]):\n        self.elements = elements\n        self.size = len(elements)\n\n\nclass DivisibilityChecker:\n    \"\"\"Provides algorithms to verify divisibility and compute the largest divisible subset.\"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_divisible(a: int, b: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Return True if a divides b or b divides a.\"\"\"\n        # The design document's DivisibilityChecker.is_divisible method shows checking if a divides b OR b divides a.\n        # However, the largest_subset algorithm (longest divisible subsequence) typically means b % a == 0 when sorted.\n        # Given the \"is_divisible(numbers[i], numbers[j])\" call within the sorted loop,\n        # where numbers[j] <= numbers[i], it implies checking numbers[i] % numbers[j] == 0.\n        # Let's adhere to the definition provided in the code snippet in the design for this method:\n        # \"return a % b == 0 or b % a == 0\"\n        # If numbers[j] is 0, this will raise ZeroDivisionError.\n        # The problem assertion [1, 3, 6, 13, 17, 18] does not include 0.\n        # Assuming input numbers are positive integers based on problem assertion.\n        if b == 0: # handle division by zero explicitly, though problem assertion suggests positive integers\n            return a == 0 # 0 is divisible by 0, a non-zero number is not divisible by 0\n        if a == 0:\n            return True # 0 is divisible by any non-zero number\n        return a % b == 0 or b % a == 0\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def compute_largest_subset(numbers: List[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Compute the size of the maximum divisible subset.\"\"\"\n        if not numbers:\n            return 0\n        if len(numbers) == 1:\n            return 1\n\n        # Sort numbers to simplify divisibility checks\n        # Smallest elements must come first for the DP approach based on \"a % b == 0\".\n        # If a subset is [1, 3, 6], sorting ensures 1 comes before 3, 3 before 6.\n        # The is_divisible check in the DP loop (numbers[i], numbers[j]) needs to consider numbers[i] % numbers[j] == 0\n        # as numbers[j] will be <= numbers[i] due to sorting.\n        # The provided `is_divisible` checks both ways. Sorting is still beneficial.\n        numbers.sort()\n        n = len(numbers)\n        dp = [1] * n  # dp[i] = size of largest subset ending with numbers[i]\n\n        for i in range(1, n):\n            for j in range(i):\n                # For sorted numbers, numbers[j] <= numbers[i].\n                # If numbers[j] == 0, then only 0 itself forms a subset of size 1.\n                # If numbers[i] == 0, it means the list has 0.\n                # The provided `is_divisible` method handles `a % b == 0 or b % a == 0`.\n                # With sorted `numbers`, `numbers[j]` is always less than or equal to `numbers[i]`.\n                # So we are effectively checking if `numbers[i] % numbers[j] == 0`.\n                if numbers[j] != 0 and numbers[i] % numbers[j] == 0: # Simplified check for sorted positive numbers\n                    dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n                elif numbers[j] == 0 and numbers[i] == 0: # special case for [0, 0, ...]\n                    dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n                elif numbers[j] == 0 and numbers[i] != 0: # 0 can divide any non-zero number\n                    # This implies [0, X] could be a subset of size 2.\n                    # Since 0 is at the beginning, dp[j] would be for 0.\n                    # If dp[j] is 1 for 0, then X can extend it.\n                    dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n                # The provided `is_divisible` logic is slightly more general:\n                # if DivisibilityChecker.is_divisible(numbers[i], numbers[j]):\n                #    dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n                # I will use the one from the design for correctness, which also handles 0 according to its own logic.\n                if DivisibilityChecker.is_divisible(numbers[i], numbers[j]):\n                    dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n\n\n        return max(dp)\n\n\ndef largest_subset(numbers: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Public function to compute the size of the largest divisible subset.\"\"\"\n    # Validate input as per design\n    if not isinstance(numbers, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a list of integers.\")\n    for idx, num in enumerate(numbers):\n        if not isinstance(num, int):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Element at index {idx} is not an integer: {num}\")\n\n    number_list = NumberList(numbers)\n    return DivisibilityChecker.compute_largest_subset(number_list.numbers)"}
