{"task_id": "Mbpp/635", "solution": "from typing import List\n\n# This helper function maintains the max-heap property for a subtree rooted at index i within arr.\n# It ensures that the parent node at index i is greater than or equal to its children.\ndef _heapify(arr: List[int], n: int, i: int):\n    largest = i\n    left = 2 * i + 1\n    right = 2 * i + 2\n\n    if left < n and arr[left] > arr[largest]:\n        largest = left\n\n    if right < n and arr[right] > arr[largest]:\n        largest = right\n\n    if largest != i:\n        arr[i], arr[largest] = arr[largest], arr[i]\n        _heapify(arr, n, largest)\n\ndef heap_sort(input_list: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a list of integers using the heap sort algorithm.\n    Returns a new list with elements sorted in ascending order, without modifying the original input_list.\n    \"\"\"\n    arr = list(input_list)\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    # Build a max-heap: Rearrange array into a max-heap from the last non-leaf node up to the root.\n    for i in range(n // 2 - 1, -1, -1):\n        _heapify(arr, n, i)\n\n    # Extract elements one by one: Move the current root (largest element) to the end of the array\n    # and then re-heapify the remaining elements.\n    for i in range(n - 1, 0, -1):\n        arr[i], arr[0] = arr[0], arr[i]  # Swap root with last element\n        _heapify(arr, i, 0)  # Call heapify on the reduced heap\n\n    return arr"}
