{"task_id": "Mbpp/799", "solution": "def left_rotate(number, d):\n    \"\"\"\n    Rotates the bits of a 32-bit number left by d positions.\n    The number is treated as an unsigned 32-bit integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    BITS = 32\n    MASK = (1 << BITS) - 1\n\n    # Normalize d to ensure it's within the range [0, BITS-1] for rotation\n    # A rotation by BITS is equivalent to a rotation by 0\n    d_normalized = d % BITS\n\n    # Perform the left rotation:\n    # 1. (number << d_normalized) shifts bits left. In Python, this can exceed 32 bits.\n    # 2. (number >> (BITS - d_normalized)) captures the bits that wrap around from the left.\n    # 3. The bitwise OR combines these two parts.\n    # 4. The final result is masked with 0xFFFFFFFF to ensure it remains a 32-bit number.\n    rotated_number = ((number << d_normalized) | (number >> (BITS - d_normalized))) & MASK\n    \n    return rotated_number"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/803", "solution": "def is_perfect_square(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given number is a perfect square.\n    Raises ValueError if the number is negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input number cannot be negative.\")\n    \n    # Calculate the integer part of the square root\n    sqrt_val = int(number**0.5)\n    \n    # A number is a perfect square if squaring its integer square root yields the original number\n    return sqrt_val * sqrt_val == number"}
